  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe extreme heat will come to an end after today as a cold front \nmoves across the FA. Dewpoints will gradually begin to lower a \nfew degrees this afternoon with mixing in the boundary layer \ntapping into a dry layer around 750 mb, followed by additional \ndry air advection overnight. A H5 impulse also rounds a 594 dm \nridge parked over the southwest Plains this evening that will \nprovide modest lift for a few scattered showers and perhaps an \nisolated thunderstorm. Have limit NBM PoPs to a 30% confined to\nSEMO as most of the CAMs keep things dry with the HRRR being \nthe most aggressive. \n\nBehind the front, a broad upper level trough sets up over the \nOhio Valley with persistent northwest flow as the aformentioned \nridge shifts more west. Daily high temperatures will range from \nthe low to mid 80s through the weekend with much needed relief \nfrom the humidity. Dewpoints mix down into the lower 60s until \nFriday. While a weak area of sfc high pressure will provide \nample dry conditions Wednesday and Thursday, daily rain chances \nmake a return Friday and continue into early next week as H5 \nenergy moving onshore over northern California this evening is \nprogged to develop into a cutoff low over the FA over the \nweekend. The greatest rain chances by the NBM peak at a 60-70% \nFriday night into Saturday, with each afternoon favoring the \nhighest risk through the remainder of the period.",
